          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What home improvement ideas add the most value?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Annual cost-versus-value surveys consistently show that exterior and maintenance projects, such as replacing an entry door or garage door, tend to recover a larger share of their cost than big interior remodels. Smaller upgrades like fresh paint, sealed gaps and updated lighting also improve how a home shows.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I fix first in an older house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Start with water and air: leaks, failed caulk, missing weatherstripping and poor drainage. These protect the structure and reduce energy waste. Then address safety items such as smoke and carbon monoxide detectors, electrical hazards and lead paint before cosmetic work.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do smart thermostats really save money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They can, but results vary. Savings depend on your climate, your equipment, how well the house is sealed and whether you actually use the scheduling features. A smart thermostat reliably prevents conditioning an empty house, and some utilities offer rebates, so check local programs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a smart lock myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In most cases yes. Many smart locks replace the deadbolt on a standard prepared door with a screwdriver in under an hour. Measure your door's bore and backset first, choose the right connectivity version, and keep a backup entry method in case batteries run out.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What basic tools does every homeowner need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A cordless drill/driver, tape measure, level, stud finder, caulk gun, utility knife, screwdriver set and pliers cover most household projects. Buy fewer, better tools on one battery platform rather than many cheap tools you rarely use.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When is the best time to buy tools and home upgrades?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Prices commonly soften around major holidays, at the end of a season and when a new product generation arrives. Watch the price of specific items for a few weeks so you recognize a real discount. Avoid buying simply because a sale is ending.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which home improvements need a permit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Requirements vary by location, but electrical, plumbing, gas, structural and some roofing, deck and fence projects commonly need permits. Cosmetic work like painting and caulking generally does not. Call your local building department before starting; it is usually a free, quick answer.
              </p>
